{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The problem, and its proven root cause\n\n`list_tickets` against this store can terminate with `MCP request failed: Transport closed` and\nno structured error payload — the caller cannot tell an invalid query from a dead server from a\nresponse that was merely too large.\n\nUnfiltered `tools/call list_tickets {}` against the deployed endpoint returned `http=200`,\n**94,541,551 bytes over 177.29 seconds**, in one well-formed SSE frame. **The server does not\nfail** — the client abandons that response, in a client-specific way (`Transport closed`, a\ntimeout, or 219,815 chars spilled to disk), and none can distinguish \"too big\" from \"server\ndied\" because the server offers no bound and no structured signal. Rejected candidates and the\nmeasurement tables are in the Phase 1 and Phase 4 comments.\n\n## Scope: this ticket is the BOUND. The projection is its prerequisite.\n\n`resistant-constant-nurseshark` (`98b8-5f08-1569-45cc`) is the lean projection, linked\n`depends_on`. **It must land first**: without it this bound would refuse even\n`list_tickets(status=\"open\")` at 224,610 lean bytes. Not greenfield: `_cap_workflow_payload`\nalready states this 90,000-byte contract (*\"under the client's ~25K-token budget\"*, WS-ffc4)\nbut is wired only to the two workflow reads; the list tools have **no size check at all**.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Cause identified and explicitly reconciled with both sibling tickets (above).\n\n- [ ] **Both list tools bound at one named budget, and REFUSE rather than truncate.**\n      `list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` bound at `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES`, aliasing the\n      existing 90,000-byte `_WORKFLOW_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES` rather than introducing a second\n      number. No partial list is ever returned: a shortened list is indistinguishable from a\n      complete one. **Verified by** a test asserting an over-budget call for each tool returns\n      no result and carries no `structuredContent`.\n\n- [ ] **The budget measures the WIRE payload, not the reducer rows.** Raw dicts under-estimate\n      — the dangerous direction, since an under-estimating bound passes something that still\n      overruns. So the bound runs INSIDE the tool body after `TicketStateOut.model_validate`\n      and calls `js_safe_result` on those rows itself, rather than waiting for the error guard,\n      which applies that transform only after the body returns: same pure function, same rows,\n      so measured bytes equal emitted bytes while the raise point stays where a structured\n      error is deliverable. At 2,855 rows the raw measurement under-reports by 178%, its\n      dominant axis being `TicketStateOut`'s DECLARED defaults re-adding every field the\n      projection dropped (figures in the comment). **Verified by** an exactness assertion:\n      measured bytes == the bytes the client gets.\n\n- [ ] **The refusal is structured, and the exception TYPE is right.** Raises a\n      `rebar._errors.RebarError` carrying `error_code=\"response_too_large\"`. The type matters,\n      not just the attribute: `_envelope_error` only envelopes a known rebar exception type, so\n      a plain `Exception` with `.error_code` is dropped and the caller gets an unstructured\n      failure. The code joins the closed vocabulary — added to `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` and\n      `tests/unit/api_surface_baseline.json`; `error_code_for` needs no change (step 5 returns\n      a non-empty `exc.error_code`) and no schema change is needed (`error_envelope.error` is\n      an open string, not an enum). **Verified by** a test asserting the raised `ToolError`'s\n      `__cause__` is an `McpEnvelopeError` whose envelope names that code, plus the api-surface\n      baseline test.\n\n- [ ] **The remedy is one the REFUSED TOOL can execute.** `list_tickets` names its six filters.\n      **`ready_tickets` accepts NO filter argument** — only `sort` and the `full` its\n      prerequisite adds, and `full` switches SHAPE, not membership — so a shared \"narrow with\n      has_tag\" message would name arguments the tool rejects. Its refusal points at\n      `next_batch(epic_id)`. The remedy has to work, because `ready_tickets` sits at ~1.7%\n      headroom after the projection (59 rows, 88,480 of 90,000 bytes) — and the budget is NOT\n      inflated to hide that, since raising it moves the failure back into the client where\n      there is no structured signal; the narrower-model follow-up is `lucky-egoistic-akitainu`\n      (`0c99-8f2b-0de7-48f2`). **Verified by** a test asserting `list_tickets`' message names\n      its filters and `ready_tickets`' names `next_batch` and NOT\n      `has_tag`/`without_tag`/`parent`.\n\n- [ ] **The extraction preserves every consumer.** The cluster moves to\n      `src/rebar/_mcp_budget.py` (a real call-graph seam — both bounds measure through\n      `_payload_bytes`; `mcp_server.py` is at 797 of its 800-LOC cap). **Three consumers\n      reference `_cap_workflow_payload` on the `rebar.mcp_server` namespace from OUTSIDE that\n      module and would break on a bare move**: `scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py:98`,\n      `tests/unit/test_gen_mcp_reference.py:122`,\n      `tests/interfaces/facades/test_workflow_mcp_schema.py:36,43,67`. All are preserved by\n      **RE-EXPORT**, not repointed, so none is edited (rejected alternative in the\n      extraction-safety comment). **Verified by** those three passing unedited plus\n      `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py`.\n\n- [ ] **Every builder of the shared tool context gains `bound_list_payload`.** The bound reaches\n      the tool bodies as a ctx member, so `register_read_tools` raises `AttributeError` against\n      any ctx lacking it — an omitted builder fails at REGISTRATION, not at assertion. NINE\n      exist and ALL are updated: `mcp_server.build_server`, `scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py`, and\n      the seven `tests/unit` fixtures `test_audit_read`, `test_bridge_projects_surfaces_heldout`,\n      `test_cross_session_mcp`, `test_gen_mcp_reference`, `test_mcp_plan_review_status`,\n      `test_plan_review_explain`, `test_read_freshness_parity`. **Verified by** running each.\n\n- [ ] **Regression coverage asserts the error's CONTENTS**, not merely that a refusal occurred:\n      `error` as its own envelope key, and match count, byte size, budget and remedy as\n      substrings WITHIN `message`, for BOTH tools. Plus an under-budget list returned whole,\n      and the regression window: 250 plain rows measuring 49,652 raw (under budget) but 135,902\n      on the wire (51% over) must still be refused. Driven through the REGISTERED\n      `CallToolRequest` handler a real client reaches, so a guard on the post-`build_server()`\n      instance alone cannot pass. **Verified by** a mutation check: neutering the budget\n      comparison must turn these RED.\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` through the\n      deployed endpoint return results (or a structured limit error) with no transport close.\n      **Auth precondition:** the endpoint uses per-client **static bearer PATs**\n      (`REBAR_MCP_AUTH_STRATEGIES=static`, `MCP_CLIENT_PAT_*` per `docs/mcp-client-setup.md`),\n      NOT an OAuth flow, so the client MUST send `Authorization: Bearer $MCP_CLIENT_PAT_…`; a\n      `401`/`403` is a FAILED validation, not an environment excuse. Evidence must be OBSERVED\n      against the deployed server and recorded in a comment naming the tools, arguments and\n      response. A passing local test does NOT satisfy this, nor does a merged change: the\n      container must have redeployed and served the call.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- `docs/release-notes.md` + `CHANGELOG.md` record the refusal as NEW behaviour, not a break:\n  it is additive, and nothing that fits today starts failing.\n- `make lint` clean (ruff + format, ratchet `new=0 increased=0`), `make typecheck` clean,\n  `pytest tests/unit tests/interfaces` with no failures.\n- `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1` on Gerrit, stacked on the prerequisite.\n- Live-validation evidence recorded as a comment on this ticket."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787963555898903001,"uuid":"dfbadcca-09d0-4e4a-9f56-9f0f37c806b9"}